---
title: "Data operations"
description: "Create, update, browse, count, and delete Qdrant points with readable QQL statements."
sidebar:
  order: 4
---

Data statements make point writes and maintenance operations inspectable before
they reach Qdrant. Use a `WHERE` selector for mutations and a `SHARD` clause
only when you are targeting a known shard key.

## Upsert a complete point

Every value object requires exactly one case-insensitive `id` key. A point can
carry payload values, named dense vectors, sparse vectors, or multivectors.

{% qqlExample title="Payload with a named dense vector" %}
```sql
UPSERT INTO docs VALUES {
  id: 1,
  title: 'QQL',
  text: 'Declarative vector retrieval',
  vector: {
    dense: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]
  }
};
```
{% /qqlExample %}

When execution owns the embedding step, use `USING` or `EMBED` directives. The
configured embedder resolves text before the operation is planned.

{% qqlExample title="Embed multiple payload fields into named vectors" %}
```sql
UPSERT INTO articles VALUES {
  id: 'article-1',
  title: 'Planning hybrid retrieval',
  body: 'Dense and sparse candidate generation'
}
EMBED body INTO dense_body USING DENSE,
      title INTO sparse_title USING SPARSE;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

## Update only what changed

Use a payload update, vector update, payload-key delete, vector delete, or
point delete rather than overwriting a full point unnecessarily.

{% qqlExample title="Targeted point mutations" %}
```sql
UPDATE docs
SET PAYLOAD = {status: 'reviewed'}
WHERE id = 42;

UPDATE docs
SET VECTOR dense = [0.2, 0.3, 0.4]
WHERE id = 42;

DELETE PAYLOAD draft_notes
FROM docs
WHERE status = 'published';

DELETE VECTOR dense
FROM docs
WHERE id = 42;

DELETE FROM docs
WHERE status = 'expired';
```
{% /qqlExample %}

{% aside type="caution" title="Make destructive selectors explicit" %}
`DELETE`, `CLEAR PAYLOAD`, `DELETE PAYLOAD`, and `DELETE VECTOR` are designed
around explicit point selectors. Inspect the compiled route in the playground
before pointing a mutation at a production endpoint.
{% /aside %}

## Browse and count

`SCROLL` is for deterministic browsing, exports, and backfills. `COUNT` is for
cardinality and accepts the same filter vocabulary.

{% qqlExample title="Browse published documents and count them" %}
```sql
SCROLL FROM docs
WHERE status = 'published'
WITH VECTOR false
LIMIT 100;

COUNT FROM docs
WHERE status = 'published'
WITH (exact = true);
```
{% /qqlExample %}

Use `SCROLL ... AFTER point_id` to continue from a cursor. Use `COUNT` without
`exact = true` when an approximate answer is sufficient for a large collection.
